1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,250
Listen to a talk in an architecture class.

2
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:08,850
Man: Have you ever thought about how ancient concrete structures like Roman amphitheatres

3
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:13,158
and aqueducts have managed to last for thousands of years,

4
00:00:13,158 --> 00:00:17,100
while modern concrete buildings seem to fall apart

5
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:20,050
and need replacing after just a few decades?

6
00:00:20,050 --> 00:00:27,220
For a long time, researchers thought the secret to Roman concrete's durability was volcanic ash.

7
00:00:27,220 --> 00:00:31,276
That makes sense, since volcanic ash was a major ingredient,

8
00:00:31,276 --> 00:00:35,600
and it was explicitly described as such by architects

9
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:38,170
and historians who were writing at the time.

10
00:00:38,170 --> 00:00:45,000
But it turns out the real secret was actually tiny chunks of calcium carbonate.

11
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,814
These formed because the Romans used dry,

12
00:00:47,814 --> 00:00:54,246
quick lime in their mix instead of the way we make concrete today called Portland cement,

13
00:00:54,246 --> 00:00:59,070
where lime gets mixed with water first, then blended with everything else.

14
00:00:59,070 --> 00:01:03,667
When cracks show up in Roman concrete and water gets in,

15
00:01:03,667 --> 00:01:09,100
those calcium carbonate bits react with the water and seal up the cracks.

16
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:12,540
So the concrete basically heals itself

17
00:01:12,560 --> 00:01:16,060
fixes the cracks, and gets stronger over time.

18
00:01:16,060 --> 00:01:20,918
Funny thing is, researchers have noticed these chunks in Roman concrete for ages,

19
00:01:20,918 --> 00:01:24,520
but they just figured they were a production flaw

20
00:01:24,520 --> 00:01:27,270
like someone didn't mix the ingredients well enough.

21
00:01:27,270 --> 00:01:32,360
Anyway, considering how short lived modern concrete is

22
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:34,938
and the fact that making it is bad for the environment,

23
00:01:34,938 --> 00:01:43,010
it's pretty exciting to think we might be able to use some of these ancient Roman tricks in future construction.
